saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11d · Step 3, theory – Hybrid search, chunking and search across tables\n\nKeyword and meaning disagreed in step 3. In practice nobody wants two lists: one search should use both, rank the results together and also work on long texts and across tables. This notebook backs the theory slide on hybrid search.\n\n**Question:** How do full text and vector search come together in one index, one query and one ranking?\n\n- **Features:** Hybrid Vector Index, `DBMS_HYBRID_VECTOR.SEARCH`, `DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN` (chunking and embedding), Ubiquitous Database Search (`DBMS_SEARCH`)\n- **Pattern:** M1 – find, then check (part 1: find, in one ranking)\n- **Data:** `CASE_NOTES` (case library, 290 notes with confirmed diagnoses), `SYMPTOM_REPORTS`, `DIAGNOSES`\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `01_run_all.sql` and **11c**. Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\nIn the live demo the hybrid search stays in the theory part. This notebook is the backing for those slides. It creates two additional search indexes on small tables (sections 1 and 4); they are not part of `01_run_all.sql`. The clean-up statements are at the end.\n\nBased on the blog posts [Hybrid Vector Index – the combination of full-text and semantic vector search](https://blogs.oracle.com/coretec/hybrid-vector-index-the-combination-of-full-text-and-semantic-vector-search), [Ubiquitous Database Search](https://blogs.oracle.com/coretec/ubiquitous-database-search) and [From raw to RAG with DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN](https://blogs.oracle.com/coretec/from-raw-to-rag-with-dbms_vector_chain).\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. Create a hybrid vector index on the case library\n\nThe case library holds 290 notes with confirmed diagnoses – a good place to show a search that uses keywords and meaning at once.\n\n**Question:** How do we index the notes for both kinds of search in one index?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A hybrid vector index is one index with two parts: an Oracle Text index for keywords and a vector index for meaning. The database splits the texts into chunks, calculates the embeddings with the in-database model and keeps both parts in sync.\n\n- `MODEL ALL_MINILM_L12_V2`: the same ONNX model as for `SYMPTOM_REPORTS` (05).\n- `VECTOR_IDXTYPE IVF`: neighbor partition index on disk; `HNSW` would need a vector memory pool (`VECTOR_MEMORY_SIZE`).\n- `LEXER`, `WORDLIST`, `STOPLIST`: the German text preferences from `06_create_text_index.sql`.\n\nThe index is built on `CASE_NOTES`, the case library of 290 notes. It has no text index yet."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "DROP INDEX IF EXISTS case_notes_hybrid_idx;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "CREATE HYBRID VECTOR INDEX case_notes_hybrid_idx\n  ON case_notes (note_text)\n  PARAMETERS ('MODEL ALL_MINILM_L12_V2\n               VECTOR_IDXTYPE IVF\n               LEXER symptom_lexer\n               WORDLIST symptom_wordlist\n               STOPLIST symptom_stoplist');"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Index status\n\n**Question:** Is the hybrid index ready, and were all notes indexed?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Like every Oracle Text index, the hybrid index appears in `CTX_USER_INDEXES`; rows that failed are listed in `CTX_USER_INDEX_ERRORS`.\n\n**Result:** the index is ready (`INDEXED`) with all 290 case notes and no errors. One `CREATE` statement built the text part, the chunks and the embeddings."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT i.idx_name,\n       i.idx_status,\n       i.idx_docid_count,\n       (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ctx_user_index_errors e WHERE e.err_index_name = i.idx_name) AS errors\nFROM   ctx_user_indexes i\nWHERE  i.idx_name = 'CASE_NOTES_HYBRID_IDX';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. One query, two searches, one ranking\n\nJonas' full report for the meaning, plus the words for \"Plopp\" and \"wobbly\" that patients use.\n\n**Question:** Which case notes rank highest when keywords and meaning count together?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`DBMS_HYBRID_VECTOR.SEARCH` takes the search as a JSON document:\n\n- `vector.search_text`: the text for the semantic search, here Jonas' complete report\n- `text.contains`: the keyword query in Oracle Text syntax: `Plopp OR Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen OR wackelig`\n- `search_fusion`: how the two result lists are combined (`UNION`, `INTERSECT`, `TEXT_ONLY`, `VECTOR_ONLY`, …)\n- `search_scorer`: `RRF` (Reciprocal Rank Fusion) combines the ranks; `RSF` (default) the normalized scores\n- `return`: the fields of the result and the number of hits (`topN`)\n\nThe result is a JSON array. `JSON_TABLE` turns it into rows, and the `ROWID` leads back to the case note.\n\n**Result:** 7 of the 10 hits are cruciate ligament ruptures, although the case library has more sprains (70) than ruptures (45). Notes with *Knall*, *geknallt* or *Schnappen* that also sound like Jonas move up (C0194, C0067, C0081, C0157, C0142). Notes with only one of the two signals still make it: C0029 and C0240 by meaning alone (text score 0), C0060 and C0252 by keyword alone (vector score 0).\n\nBut rank 1 is a sprain: C0199 sounds most like Jonas – hiking, twisted, swollen, pain when walking – and it contains the keyword \"Plopp\": *\"Kein Plopp, nichts Auffälliges\"*. The hybrid search combines two signals, and neither of them understands a negation. One ranking, better candidates – but still no judgement."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT c.case_id,\n       ROUND(jt.score, 2)         AS score,\n       ROUND(jt.text_score, 2)    AS text_score,\n       ROUND(jt.vector_score, 2)  AS vector_score,\n       c.diagnosis_label,\n       c.note_text\nFROM   JSON_TABLE(\n         DBMS_HYBRID_VECTOR.SEARCH(JSON('{\n           \"hybrid_index_name\" : \"CASE_NOTES_HYBRID_IDX\",\n           \"search_scorer\"     : \"rrf\",\n           \"search_fusion\"     : \"UNION\",\n           \"vector\" : { \"search_text\" : \"Knie beim Wandern verdreht, geschwollen, tut beim Gehen weh. Beim Abstieg hat es kurz Plopp gemacht, jetzt fühlt sich das Knie auf den Stufen wackelig an.\" },\n           \"text\"   : { \"contains\"    : \"Plopp OR Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen OR wackelig\" },\n           \"return\" : { \"values\" : [\"rowid\", \"score\", \"text_score\", \"vector_score\"],\n                        \"topN\"   : 10 }\n         }')),\n         '$[*]'\n         COLUMNS (doc_rowid     VARCHAR2(18) PATH '$.rowid',\n                  score         NUMBER       PATH '$.score',\n                  text_score    NUMBER       PATH '$.text_score',\n                  vector_score  NUMBER       PATH '$.vector_score')) jt\nJOIN   case_notes c ON c.ROWID = CHARTOROWID(jt.doc_rowid)\nORDER  BY jt.score DESC, c.case_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Compare the fusion modes\n\nHow the two result lists are combined is a choice, and it changes the answer.\n\n**Question:** How does the ranking change with keywords only, meaning only, or both?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same search with four fusion modes. The search document is built with `JSON_OBJECT`, so the mode comes from a column: the table value constructor `VALUES` provides one row per mode, and `JSON_TABLE` runs the search for each row (lateral join).\n\n**Result:** in the case library every mode puts the cruciate ligament rupture first:\n\n| Mode | Ruptures | Sprains |\n|---|---|---|\n| `TEXT_ONLY` | 8 | 2 |\n| `INTERSECT` | 8 | 2 |\n| `UNION` | 7 | 3 |\n| `VECTOR_ONLY` | 6 | 3 |\n\nThe keywords are the most precise signal here – because the keyword list already contains the right synonyms (*Knall*, *geknallt*, *Schnappen*); someone had to know them. `UNION` lies between its two inputs: fusion makes one ranking out of two lists, it does not automatically beat the best single signal. And every mode still has sprains among its first 10. Even the best ranking only delivers candidates – the decision comes from the knowledge graph (11e)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH modes AS (\n  SELECT fusion\n  FROM   (VALUES ('TEXT_ONLY'), ('VECTOR_ONLY'), ('UNION'), ('INTERSECT')) m (fusion)\n),\nhits AS (\n  SELECT m.fusion,\n         jt.doc_rowid\n  FROM   modes m,\n         JSON_TABLE(\n           DBMS_HYBRID_VECTOR.SEARCH(\n             JSON_OBJECT('hybrid_index_name' VALUE 'CASE_NOTES_HYBRID_IDX',\n                         'search_scorer'     VALUE 'rrf',\n                         'search_fusion'     VALUE m.fusion,\n                         'vector'            VALUE JSON_OBJECT('search_text' VALUE 'Knie beim Wandern verdreht, geschwollen, tut beim Gehen weh. Beim Abstieg hat es kurz Plopp gemacht, jetzt fühlt sich das Knie auf den Stufen wackelig an.'),\n                         'text'              VALUE JSON_OBJECT('contains'    VALUE 'Plopp OR Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen OR wackelig'),\n                         'return'            VALUE JSON_OBJECT('values' VALUE JSON_ARRAY('rowid', 'score'),\n                                                               'topN'   VALUE 10)\n                         RETURNING JSON)),\n           '$[*]' COLUMNS (doc_rowid VARCHAR2(18) PATH '$.rowid')) jt\n)\nSELECT h.fusion,\n       COUNT(*)                                                        AS hits,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN c.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur'     THEN 1 END) AS acl_ruptures,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN c.diagnosis_code = 'kniedistorsion' THEN 1 END) AS knee_sprains,\n       STATS_MODE(c.diagnosis_label)                                   AS most_frequent_diagnosis\nFROM   hits h\nJOIN   case_notes c ON c.ROWID = CHARTOROWID(h.doc_rowid)\nGROUP  BY h.fusion\nORDER  BY h.fusion;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. From raw text to chunks and vectors: `DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN`\n\nJonas' report has two sentences: the first fits many knee injuries, the second sounds like something worse. A RAG pipeline cuts texts into such chunks before it embeds them.\n\n**Question:** How do we split a text into chunks and calculate a vector for each?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A hybrid index chunks and embeds automatically. `DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN` exposes the same pipeline step by step, for example for a RAG application:\n\n1. `UTL_TO_CHUNKS` splits a text into chunks. Parameters: `by` (unit), `max` (maximum size), `overlap`, `split` (where to cut, here at sentence boundaries) and `normalize`.\n2. `UTL_TO_EMBEDDINGS` calculates one vector per chunk, here with the in-database model (`\"provider\" : \"database\"`).\n\nBoth return a collection of JSON documents; `TABLE(…)` and `JSON_TABLE` turn them into rows.\n\n**Result:** Jonas' report becomes two chunks, cut at the sentence boundary: chunk 1 (characters 1–60) *\"Knie beim Wandern verdreht, geschwollen, tut beim Gehen weh.\"* and chunk 2 (from character 62, 93 characters) *\"Beim Abstieg hat es kurz Plopp gemacht, jetzt fühlt sich das Knie auf den Stufen wackelig an.\"* The first sentence describes what many knee injuries have in common, the second the telling details."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT jt.chunk_id,\n       jt.chunk_offset,\n       jt.chunk_length,\n       jt.chunk_data\nFROM   symptom_reports s,\n       TABLE(DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN.UTL_TO_CHUNKS(\n               TO_CLOB(s.report_text),\n               JSON('{\"by\" : \"words\", \"max\" : \"20\", \"overlap\" : \"0\", \"split\" : \"sentence\", \"normalize\" : \"all\"}'))) c,\n       JSON_TABLE(c.column_value, '$'\n         COLUMNS (chunk_id      NUMBER         PATH '$.chunk_id',\n                  chunk_offset  NUMBER         PATH '$.chunk_offset',\n                  chunk_length  NUMBER         PATH '$.chunk_length',\n                  chunk_data    VARCHAR2(4000) PATH '$.chunk_data')) jt\nWHERE  s.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'\nORDER  BY jt.chunk_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Which chunk sounds like which diagnosis?\n\n**Question:** Which diagnosis does each sentence of Jonas' report resemble?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Each chunk gets its own vector and its own three nearest FHIR reports. `OUTER APPLY` runs the nearest-neighbour search once per chunk (lateral join, no cross product).\n\nA whole report has one embedding, an average of its meaning. Split into sentences, the first sentence (*twisted, swollen, pain when walking*) describes what many knee injuries have in common, the second (*Plopp, wobbly on the steps*) the typical signs of a cruciate ligament rupture. Does chunking make the decisive detail visible?\n\n**Result:** no. Chunk 1 finds meniscus lesions and a sprain – plausible for a sentence that fits many knee injuries. But chunk 2 finds two contusions and a medial ligament strain, not one cruciate ligament rupture. The nearest report (P0602-B2) shares *Abstieg* and the stairs, not the findings. The model does not know that *Plopp* and *wackelig* are the colloquial words for an audible pop and instability – in 11c, section 9, the words *geknallt* and *gibt nach* found the ruptures at once.\n\nChunking can only make visible what the embedding model understands. Which words stand for which finding is knowledge – and that is what the knowledge graph in 11e adds."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH chunks AS (\n  SELECT et.embed_id                AS chunk_id,\n         et.embed_data              AS chunk_text,\n         TO_VECTOR(et.embed_vector) AS chunk_vector\n  FROM   symptom_reports s,\n         TABLE(DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN.UTL_TO_EMBEDDINGS(\n                 DBMS_VECTOR_CHAIN.UTL_TO_CHUNKS(\n                   TO_CLOB(s.report_text),\n                   JSON('{\"by\" : \"words\", \"max\" : \"20\", \"overlap\" : \"0\", \"split\" : \"sentence\", \"normalize\" : \"all\"}')),\n                 JSON('{\"provider\" : \"database\", \"model\" : \"ALL_MINILM_L12_V2\"}'))) e,\n         JSON_TABLE(e.column_value, '$[*]'\n           COLUMNS (embed_id      NUMBER         PATH '$.embed_id',\n                    embed_data    VARCHAR2(4000) PATH '$.embed_data',\n                    embed_vector  CLOB           PATH '$.embed_vector')) et\n  WHERE  s.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'\n)\nSELECT c.chunk_id,\n       c.chunk_text,\n       n.report_id,\n       n.distance,\n       n.diagnosis_label\nFROM   chunks c\nOUTER  APPLY (SELECT s.report_id,\n                     ROUND(VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, c.chunk_vector, COSINE), 4) AS distance,\n                     d.diagnosis_label\n              FROM   symptom_reports s\n              LEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n              WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n              ORDER  BY VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, c.chunk_vector, COSINE)\n              FETCH  FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY) n\nORDER  BY c.chunk_id, n.distance;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. One search across several tables: Ubiquitous Database Search\n\nCase notes and diagnoses live in different tables, and a user does not care which.\n\n**Question:** Can one search find a term across several tables?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`DBMS_SEARCH` creates one search index over several tables or views. Each row becomes a virtual JSON document; the index table has the name of the index and the columns `METADATA` (owner, source table, key) and `DATA`. New and changed rows are indexed on commit.\n\nHere: the case library `CASE_NOTES` and the diagnoses `DIAGNOSES` in one index `DOAG_SEARCH_IDX`. The first block removes an index from an earlier run."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "BEGIN\n  DBMS_SEARCH.DROP_INDEX('DOAG_SEARCH_IDX');\nEXCEPTION\n  WHEN OTHERS THEN NULL;   -- index does not exist yet\nEND;\n/"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "BEGIN\n  DBMS_SEARCH.CREATE_INDEX(index_name => 'DOAG_SEARCH_IDX');\n  DBMS_SEARCH.ADD_SOURCE(index_name => 'DOAG_SEARCH_IDX', source_name => 'CASE_NOTES');\n  DBMS_SEARCH.ADD_SOURCE(index_name => 'DOAG_SEARCH_IDX', source_name => 'DIAGNOSES');\nEND;\n/"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Hits per source table\n\n**Question:** How many hits does each table contribute?\n\n**How the query answers it:** One `CONTAINS` over all sources. The index uses the default (English) lexer, so German compounds are not split: `Kreuzband%` (wildcard) finds *Kreuzbandruptur*, a search for `Kreuzband` alone would not.\n\n**Result:** one query, two tables: 45 hits in `CASE_NOTES` and 80 in `DIAGNOSES`. Both numbers are exactly the cruciate ligament ruptures – the 45 of the case library and the 80 diagnoses from 11a. The index covers all columns of a row, so the hits come from the diagnosis label, not from the patients' words: nobody writes *Kreuzbandruptur* in a symptom report."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT JSON_VALUE(metadata, '$.SOURCE') AS source_table,\n       COUNT(*)                         AS hits\nFROM   doag_search_idx\nWHERE  CONTAINS(data, 'Kreuzband%') > 0\nGROUP  BY JSON_VALUE(metadata, '$.SOURCE')\nORDER  BY source_table;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Search one column and look at the virtual document\n\n**Question:** How does a hit look as a document, and how do we search one column only?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`JSON_TEXTCONTAINS` restricts the search to one path, `$.<OWNER>.<TABLE>.<COLUMN>`. `DBMS_SEARCH.GET_DOCUMENT` assembles the virtual JSON document of a hit on the fly.\n\n**Result:** the metadata identifies each row by owner, table and primary key; the virtual document contains all columns of the row under `DOAG2026` → `CASE_NOTES`. Two of the three hits are cruciate ligament ruptures (*\"Ich habe einen Knall gehört\"*). The third, C0073, is a sprain: *\"Kein Knall gehört\"* – the negation once more, now in the case library."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT JSON_SERIALIZE(metadata)                                                    AS row_identity,\n       JSON_SERIALIZE(DBMS_SEARCH.GET_DOCUMENT('DOAG_SEARCH_IDX', metadata) PRETTY) AS virtual_document\nFROM   doag_search_idx\nWHERE  JSON_TEXTCONTAINS(data, '$.DOAG2026.CASE_NOTES.NOTE_TEXT', 'Knall')\nORDER  BY JSON_VALUE(metadata, '$.KEY.CASE_ID')\nFETCH  FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- A **hybrid vector index** keeps keywords and meaning in one index; `DBMS_HYBRID_VECTOR.SEARCH` returns one ranking with both scores.\n- **Chunking** shows which part of a text carries which meaning.\n- **Ubiquitous Database Search** searches several tables with one `CONTAINS`.\n- All of this finds candidates. Whether a candidate fits is decided in **11e** by rules in the knowledge graph.\n\n### Clean-up (optional)\n\nThe two indexes are not needed for the other notebooks. To remove them, run these statements, for example in a new cell:\n\n```sql\nDROP INDEX IF EXISTS case_notes_hybrid_idx;\nEXEC DBMS_SEARCH.DROP_INDEX('DOAG_SEARCH_IDX')\n```"
    languageId: markdown
